===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}}
## To [[give#Verb|give]] [[power#Noun|power]], [[strength#Noun|strength]], or [[vigour]] to (oneself or someone, or to something); to [[strengthen]].
##* {{RQ:Wodehouse Jeeves in the Offing|chapter=XXI|passage="And do you realize that in a few shakes I've got to show up at dinner and have Mrs Cream being very, very kind to me? It hurts the pride of the Woosters, Jeeves." / "My advice, sir, would be to '''fortify''' yourself for the ordeal." / "How?" / "There are always cocktails, sir. Should I pour you another?" / "You should."}}
## To [[support#Verb|support]] (one's or someone's [[opinion#Noun|opinion]], [[statement#Noun|statement]], etc.) by [[produce#Verb|producing]] [[evidence#Noun|evidence]], etc.; to [[confirm#Adjective|confirm]], to [[corroborate]].
## To [[increase#Verb|increase]] the [[nutritional]] [[value#Noun|value]] of ([[food]]) by [[add#Verb|adding]] [[ingredient]]s, especially [[mineral#Noun|minerals]] or [[vitamin]]s. {{defdate|from mid 20th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|Soy milk is often '''fortified''' with calcium.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1979|journal=Kiplinger's Personal Finance|volume=33|issue=7|month=July|page=47|passage=Compare the nutrition information label of a regular ready-to-eat '''fortified''' cereal with that of a presweetened brand and you'll note that, although the sweetened one's sugar content is higher, the fortification is virtually identical.|footer={{small|An adjective use.}}}}
## {{lb|en|figurative}}
### To [[impart]] [[fortitude]] or [[moral#Adjective|moral]] strength to (someone or their [[determination]], or something); to [[encourage]].
###* {{RQ:Scott Guy Mannering|passage=Pride came to the aid of fancy, and both combined to '''fortify''' his resolution.}}
###* {{RQ:Kipling Letters of Travel|chapter=Dead Kings|page=261|passage=Even the sight of a very great king indeed, sarcophagused under electric light in a hall full of most '''fortifying''' pictures, does not hold him [a visitor to the Valley of the Kings, Egypt] too long.|footer=An adjective use.}}
### To make (something) [[defensible]] against [[attack#Noun|attack]] by [[hostile#Adjective|hostile]] [[force#Noun|forces]].
## {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[make#Verb|make]] (something) [[structurally]] [[strong#Adjective|strong]]; to strengthen.
## {{lb|en|military}}
### To [[increase#Verb|increase]] the [[defence#Noun|defences]] of (an [[army]], [[soldier#Noun|soldiers]], etc.), or [[put#Verb|put]] it or them in a [[defensive#Adjective|defensive]] [[position#Noun|position]].
### To [[secure#Verb|secure]] and strengthen (a [[place#Noun|place]], its [[wall#Noun|walls]], etc.) by [[install#Verb|installing]] [[fortification]]s or other [[military#Adjective|military]] [[work#Noun|works]]. {{defdate|from early 15th c.}}
### {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[provide]] (a [[city]], a [[fortress#Noun|fortress]], an army, etc.) with [[equipment]] or soldiers.
## {{lb|en|winemaking}} To [[add#Verb|add]] [[spirits#Noun|spirits]] to ([[wine#Noun|wine]]) to increase the [[alcohol]] [[content#Noun|content]]. {{defdate|from late 19th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|Sherry is made by '''fortifying''' wine.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}}
## {{lb|en|military}} To install fortifications or other military works; also {{lb|en|sometimes|figurative}}, to put up a defensive position.
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[become]] strong; to strengthen.

